

🌱 Terraform Lifecycle, Variables & Versions


---

⚙️ 1. Terraform Resource Lifecycle

Terraform lifecycle settings define how resources are created, updated, or destroyed.
You use the lifecycle block inside a resource.


---

🧩 Example:

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
    prevent_destroy       = true
    ignore_changes        = [tags]
  }
}


---

🔹 Lifecycle Arguments

Argument	Description

create_before_destroy	Creates a new resource before deleting the old one (avoids downtime).
prevent_destroy	Prevents accidental deletion — Terraform will throw an error if you try to destroy it.
ignore_changes	Ignores certain attributes during updates (e.g., tags, security groups).



---

🧠 Example Use Case

When you want zero downtime while replacing an instance → use create_before_destroy.

When you want to protect critical infra → use prevent_destroy.



---

🧮 2. Variables in Terraform

Variables make Terraform configurations dynamic and reusable.
They are declared with the variable block.


---

🔹 Types of Variables

Type	Description	Example

Input Variables	User-defined values passed into Terraform.	Declared using variable block.
Output Variables	Values returned after deployment.	Declared using output block.
Local Variables	Temporary internal variables within a file.	Declared using locals block.



---

📘 Input Variables

Syntax:

variable "instance_type" {
  description = "Type of EC2 instance"
  type        = string
  default     = "t2.micro"
}

Usage in Resource:

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
  instance_type = var.instance_type
}


---

💡 Ways to Pass Variable Values

Method	Command / File	Description

Default value	In variable block	Used if no other value given
From CLI	-var flag	terraform apply -var="instance_type=t3.micro"
From file	.tfvars or .auto.tfvars	Example: terraform.tfvars
From environment	export TF_VAR_instance_type="t2.micro"	For shell-based environments



---

📘 Example: terraform.tfvars

instance_type = "t3.micro"
region        = "us-east-1"

Terraform automatically reads this file when applying.


---

📘 Output Variables

Syntax:

output "public_ip" {
  description = "Public IP of EC2 instance"
  value       = aws_instance.example.public_ip
}

Usage:

terraform output public_ip


---

📘 Local Variables

Used for intermediate calculations or simplifications.

Syntax:

locals {
  common_tags = {
    Owner = "Vamsi"
    Env   = "Dev"
  }
}

Usage:

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0abcd1234"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags          = local.common_tags
}


---

🧩 3. Variable Types

Type	Example	Description

string	"t2.micro"	Text values
number	3	Numeric values
bool	true or false	Boolean
list	["dev", "test", "prod"]	Ordered collection
map	{region="us-east-1", size="t2.micro"}	Key-value pairs
object	{ name = "vpc1", cidr = "10.0.0.0/16" }	Complex structures
any	Can accept any type	Flexible



---

🧠 Example: Multiple Variable Types

variable "ports" {
  type = list(number)
  default = [22, 80, 443]
}

variable "tags" {
  type = map(string)
  default = {
    Env   = "Dev"
    Owner = "Vamsi"
  }
}


---

🧩 4. Terraform Versions & Providers

Terraform uses providers to connect with different platforms (AWS, Azure, GCP, etc.).
Version constraints help keep environments consistent.


---

📘 Provider Version Example

terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}


---

📘 Terraform Version Example

terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.5.0"
}

✅ Ensures your project runs only with compatible Terraform versions.


---

🔢 Version Operators

Operator	Meaning	Example

=	Exact version	= 5.0.0
>=	Greater than or equal to	>= 1.0.0
<=	Less than or equal to	<= 2.0.0
~>	Allows updates within minor versions	~> 5.0 → 5.0.x only
!=	Not equal to version	!= 1.2.0



---

⚙️ 5. Constants (in Terraform)

Terraform doesn’t have direct “constants,”
but you can simulate constants using locals — values that don’t change.

Example:

locals {
  project_name = "DevOpsProject"
  region       = "us-east-1"
}

✅ You can’t override these from outside — so they act like constants.


---

🧩 Summary

Concept	Description

Lifecycle	Controls creation, update, and destroy behavior
Variables	Makes infra reusable and dynamic
Outputs	Displays or exports results
Locals	Internal constants or calculations
Version Constraints	Ensures compatibility with Terraform and providers



---

🚀 Quick Practice Flow

terraform init
terraform validate
terraform plan -var="instance_type=t3.micro"
terraform apply
terraform output
terraform destroy


